"Against Locke he offered a small story. Suppose everyone has a box with something in it, which we call a beetle. No one can look into anyone elses box, and each says that he knows what a beetle is only from his own. It would be quite possible for everyone to have something different in his box, or for the thing to keep changing, or for a box to be empty. The word beetle would go on being used all the same. The thing in the box, Wittgenstein says, has no place in the language-game at all. It drops out as irrelevant. If the meaning of pain were a private object that only I can inspect, the same would follow for pain. Since we plainly do talk about pain and understand one another, the word must get its meaning elsewhere, from the cries, the winces, the comforting, and the shared life in which it is used.": "b96fbe6a8e35",
"Chinese philosophers had argued about names for two centuries before the first empire. The logician Gongsun Long defended the claim that a white horse is not a horse, on the ground that horse names a shape and white a color, so that the two terms do not mean the same. Thinkers who wanted language to serve government found such puzzles dangerous. Xunzi replied with the clearest statement of conventionalism in the ancient world: Names have no fixed rightness. They are fixed by agreement. When the agreement is settled and the custom formed, they are called right, and what departs from the agreement is called wrong. A name is correct because a community uses it so. For Xunzi it follows that keeping names stable is a public duty, and that those who play with them unsettle everything built on them.": "d249097dee3d",
"Each name stands for an object.": "ca7b6befaa44",
"He defended the claim on the ground that horse names a shape.": "43006fd0ed84",
"He spent the book taking Augustines account apart.": "5135c074b580",
"In 1892 the logician Gottlob Frege showed that meaning cannot simply be the thing named either. The morning star and the evening star are one object, the planet Venus. Yet the morning star is the evening star was a discovery of astronomy, while the morning star is the morning star tells us nothing. Two names for one thing can differ in meaning. Frege concluded that a name has a sense, a way in which it presents its object, as well as a reference. Sense, he insisted, is public. It is what a whole community grasps, and no one keeps it in his head as a private image.": "8ff56f4ba63a",
"In using words we lean on one another.": "15c1044ee1d6",
"John Locke moved meaning indoors. Words, in their primary or immediate signification, stand for nothing but the ideas in the mind of him that uses them. I have an idea of gold, I attach the sound gold to it, and when I speak, the sound calls up the matching idea in you. The link between sound and idea is perfectly arbitrary, which is why there are many languages. This explains how words for absent or imaginary things can have meaning. It leaves a difficulty that Locke saw. I can never look into your mind to check that your idea matches mine. If meaning is a private item, no one can know that anyone else means what he means.": "ef03ae225f3d",
"Ludwig Wittgenstein began his last book by quoting Augustines account of learning to speak, and spent the book taking it apart. He asked his readers to stop looking for the thing a word stands for and to look at what people do with it. For a large class of cases, he wrote, the meaning of a word is its use in the language. Words are like the tools in a toolbox, a hammer, a ruler, a pot of glue, and are as various in their work. To know what five means is to be able to count, and to know what hello means is to know when to say it.": "ab46a4c89b55",
"Meaning is use is closer to a direction for looking than to a theory, and it has costs. If a word means whatever a community does with it, it becomes hard to say how a whole community could use a word wrongly, and yet people once applied fish to whales. In the 1970s Saul Kripke and Hilary Putnam argued that some words are tied to the world in a way that use does not capture. Water named a certain stuff long before anyone knew its chemistry, and what the word meant all along depended on what that stuff really is. Meanings, as Putnam put it, are not entirely in the head, and on his account they are not entirely in our practices either. The present view is a division of labor. Use and agreement explain most words, and the world itself helps to fix the meaning of some. Almost no one now defends the private image in the mind that Locke began with.": "906ad2d89da4",
"Names have no fixed rightness. They are fixed by agreement. When the agreement is settled and the custom formed, they are called right, and what departs from the agreement is called wrong. Names have no fixed object. An object is fixed for them by agreement, and when the agreement is settled and the custom formed, they are called the names of those objects. The fish trap exists for the sake of the fish. Once you have the fish, you can forget the trap. The snare exists for the sake of the rabbit. Once you have the rabbit, you can forget the snare. Words exist for the sake of meaning. Once you have the meaning, you can forget the words. Where can I find a man who has forgotten words, so that I can have a word with him?": "96696ec90757",
"School of Names": "b4f3db612fc8",
"The Buddha treated words as tools and warned against two ways of misusing them. One discourse puts the rule briefly: Dont insist on popular definitions and dont overstep normal labels. The same thing, it explains, is called a cup in one district and a bowl, a jar, or a dish in others. A person who insists on his own word and says, This is the only truth, anything else is futile, has made the first mistake. The right course is to speak as the people there speak, thinking, It seems that the venerables are referring to this. The second mistake is to throw away ordinary usage because one has seen through it. The Buddha held that words like I and person name nothing lasting, and he went on using them. A verse says of the awakened that skillful, understanding the worlds labels, theyd use these terms as mere expressions.": "099aabbc6ce9",
"The Daoists drew the opposite lesson from the same fact. If names are our arrangement and nothing more, they cannot reach what was there before any arrangement. The Daodejing opens: The name that can be named is not the constant name. Zhuangzi put it as an image. The fish trap exists for the sake of the fish. Once you have the fish, you can forget the trap. Words exist for the sake of meaning. Once you have the meaning, you can forget the words. Where can I find a man who has forgotten words, so that I can have a word with him?": "77925838295c",
"The most natural answer was given by Augustine, remembering how he learned to speak. When they named any thing, and as they spoke turned towards it, I saw and remembered that they called what they would point out by the name they uttered. Words are names, and each name stands for an object. To learn a language is to learn which name goes with which thing, and a sentence is a string of such names.": "37f8f7ed052c",
"The picture cannot be argued away at one stroke.": "6b92d6cec2da",
"The picture fits dog and table. It fits less well when we try other words. Nothing can be pointed at for five, because, perhaps, or hello. And pointing itself has to be understood. If I point at a rabbit running past and say gavagai, you cannot tell from the gesture whether I am naming the animal, its color, its running, the number one, or the act of pointing. A child who learns from pointing must already know a good deal about what kind of thing is likely to be meant.": "94ccc6d488cb",
"The sound calls up the matching idea in you.": "d4a2ca9219f3",
"The thing in the box drops out as irrelevant.": "3eb119f31b4f",
"The word would go on being used all the same.": "753725b4bd5c",
"This is close to Wittgenstein and goes a step beyond him. Both hold that a word has meaning through its use in a shared practice and that trouble starts when we take the word to name a hidden thing. For Wittgenstein the trouble is philosophical confusion. For the Buddha it is suffering. To hear I and suppose a fixed owner of experience behind it is, in his analysis, the root of craving and fear. The later tradition made this into a doctrine. Things exist as dependent designations: chariot is a name applied to wheels, axle, and frame, and person a name applied to body and mind, and neither names an extra thing (Dharma and Western Philosophy D3). Zhuangzis fish trap has its Buddhist twins in the raft that is left on the shore and the finger that points at the moon.": "3fc1ce91db6a",
"When they named any thing, and as they spoke turned towards it, I saw and remembered that they called what they would point out by the name they uttered. And that they meant this thing and no other was plain from the motion of their body, the natural language, as it were, of all nations, expressed by the countenance, glances of the eye, gestures of the limbs, and tones of the voice, indicating the affections of the mind, as it pursues, possesses, rejects, or shuns. And thus by constantly hearing words, as they occurred in various sentences, I collected gradually for what they stood.": "abcb14410a4f",
"Where can I find such a man, so that I can have a word with him?": "bf6f6d6973b2",
"You may still hold your ground. Locke would.": "6e40fde26fc9",
